lection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482500 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482500 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482511 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le xy\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le xy+3\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482515 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482530 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use 