gned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x-y\\le 3,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482540 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482547 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482580 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y+z\\le 2,\\,1\\le x+4z\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482599 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162140" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482606 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482615 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482616 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
